Pick what fits you — I recommend a digital calendar as the backbone, plus one optional physical planner or a note app for quick jotting.

1) Quick digital setup (Google Calendar / Apple / Outlook)
- Create a dedicated calendar called “Birthdays & Milestones.”
- Add each event as a recurring annual event (e.g., David — Jasper (born Apr 2023) — recurring every Apr 15).
- In the event details include:
  - Exact DOB, relation, contact info (link to contact), and gift ideas/notes.
  - Add a description like: “Order card by 4/1; ship by 4/8” or “Send flowers? + budget.”
- Set multiple reminders for each event:
  - 3–4 weeks before (order gifts / plan)
  - 1 week before (buy/gift wrap)
  - 1 day before (final reminder)
  - Morning of (push notification)
- Use Email + Push (choose both) so you don’t miss them.
- Share the calendar with family/partner (Google: Settings > Share with specific people).

2) Useful reminder cadence (template)
- Birthdays: 1 month (plan), 1 week (buy), 1 day (remind), day-of (celebrate)
- Milestones (baby): 1 month, 3 months, 6 months, 1 year — set events as single occurrence and optional recurring “annual milestone” if desired.

3) Contact integration
- Add birthdays and notes into each person’s contact card (phone Contacts). Syncs with many calendars automatically.
- Save addresses in contacts (so you can print a card label quickly).

4) Gift & task management
- Add a checklist to the calendar event or use a simple app:
  - Google Keep / Apple Reminders — create a checklist per person.
  - Trello or Notion — board with cards for each person (columns: Ideas, To Buy, Purchased, Sent).
  - Giftster or Elfster — for group gift tracking with family.
- Add shipping deadlines as reminders so things arrive on time.

5) Automations (optional, useful if many events)
- Use Zapier/IFTTT to create tasks from calendar events, or to add Slack/Slack DMs reminders.
- Integrate calendar with Gmail so an email reminder appears in your inbox.

6) Offline / physical options
- Wall calendar or planner: write birthdays and color-code (family/friends/work).
- Keep a small notebook or an index card file with names, DOB, and gift ideas (works well next to your desk).

7) Apps that help
- Google Calendar (cross-platform)
- Apple Calendar (if you’re in Apple ecosystem)
- Microsoft Outlook (if you use Microsoft)
- Birthdays for Android / Countdown+ / Birthday Reminder — lightweight birthday reminder apps
- Notion or Trello — good for lists and gift tracking

8) Privacy & newborns
- For newborns, avoid posting sensitive details publicly. Store DOB and notes in private calendar or locked notes.

9) Yearly review & backup
- Once a year (e.g., Jan), review the calendar: update contacts, add newly-borns, remove obsolete reminders.
- Export/backup your calendar (Google Calendar > Settings > Export) so you don’t lose data.

